// A call that waits for the network or for time blocks the worker, and does not
// raise, where the task cannot suspend (spec/04-semantics.md, Contexts where a
// task cannot suspend). Here the call is readline() as the entry of a task: the
// entry is a bound native. A process outside the program connects at once and
// writes one second later, so the entry task holds its worker for that time.
// `ticker` of the same worker runs after it, so the time it sees since the start
// of the program is at least half a second. The peer is a process, not a task
// of another worker: the main worker is blocked while it waits, and a program
// whose progress needs two workers to run at the same time is not portable
// (spec/README.md, Conformance, Parallel workers). The probe needs `bash` with
// its /dev/tcp redirection on the local host.
// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 10
// COVERS: suspend-context native-entry
// CHECK: true
var start = time();
var srv = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var me = task.self();
var peer = spawn("bash", ["-c", "exec 3<>/dev/tcp/127.0.0.1/" + str(srv.port()) +
                                "; sleep 1; echo late >&3; sleep 0.2"]);
var conn = srv.accept();
fun ticker() { print time() - start >= 0.5; task.send(me, "done"); }
task.spawn(conn.readline);
task.spawn(ticker);
task.receive();
peer.wait();
